The primary alternative to using the spread operator (`...`) when building or extending objects is **`Object.assign`**.

```js
// Using Object.assign (more verbose)
const newObject = Object.assign({}, oldObject, {
  overridden: 'property',
});
```

This pattern copies all enumerable own properties from `oldObject` into a new empty object, then adds/overwrites the `overridden` property. It achieves the same effect as the spread syntax:

```js
// Using spread (preferred)
const newObject = {
  ...oldObject,
  overridden: 'property',
};
```

So, if you need a fallback or are working in an environment that doesn't support ES2018 spread syntax, `Object.assign` is the documented alternative.

